course: everyday
lesson: 339
title: Borrowing without asking
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Noah in Melbourne took a toy from his cousin's house because his cousin had so many and he had few. His aunt rang to ask if anyone had seen it, and Mum was embarrassed in front of her sister. Noah posted it back with a sorry letter he wrote himself. Having less does not make taking fair.
explain: He went to confession and felt clean and light. But a few weeks later, bored at the swap club, he slipped the rare card from the pile when nobody was looking. The owner noticed straight away and the club stopped for a week for everyone, because nobody felt safe to swap any more. Shame again, and a second confession.
explain: The priest did not scold him for coming back. He said everyone slips and forgiveness does not run out, but each time you must put it right, and habits change slowly. Noah put the card back, owned up, and from then on swapped only when both boys said yes.
explain: «Borrowing» without asking is still taking: Dad's charger taken to school and lost meant Dad's phone died at work and he missed a call. Asking first costs a few seconds; not asking can cost someone else a lot.
explain: And sometimes asking gets you more: the neighbour whose apples Noah took on a dare said, «Next time just ask», and gave him a whole bag.
fact: Taking something without asking is taking, even if you mean to give it back.
fact: Having less than someone else does not make taking from them fair.
fact: A library book must be signed out so the library knows where it is and others can borrow it.
fact: Coins thrown into a wishing fountain are usually collected for charity, so they still belong to someone.
fact: Fruit from a neighbour's tree belongs to the neighbour, even if a branch hangs over your fence.
fact: A swap means both people agree to it.
fact: If a grown-up says no to buying something, taking it anyway makes two wrongs, not one.
fact: When something has been taken from a shop, the fair way is to go back with a grown-up, give it back and say sorry yourself.
fact: People who have confessed can slip again; forgiveness does not run out, but each wrong must be put right.
fact: Habits change slowly: old habits come back when you are bored or with the same friends, so fewer bad influences and more good things to do help.
fact: When you lose something you borrowed, the fair thing is to say so and help replace it.
exercise: Why did Noah take his cousin's toy? | He envied his cousin for having so many | He thought it was his own toy that he had left there | His cousin gave it to him as a gift | His aunt told him to keep it
exercise: How did Noah put the cousin's toy right? | He posted it back with a sorry letter he wrote himself | He kept it, because his cousin had plenty of toys and would never miss one | He asked Mum to say sorry for him | He hid it until the visit was forgotten
exercise: A boy grabs a library book because the queue is long, and forgets to sign it out. What goes wrong? | The library thinks it is lost and others can't borrow it | Nothing goes wrong, because library books are for everyone | The book becomes his after a week | The librarian is happy with the shorter queue
exercise: A girl fishes coins out of a shopping-centre fountain. Why is that wrong? | The coins are collected for charity and still belong to someone | Coins thrown in water are lost forever, so anyone may keep them | Only grown-ups may take coins | Fountain coins are fake
exercise: Apples hang over the fence from the neighbour's tree. What is the right thing to do if you want one? | Ask the neighbour first | Take only the ones on your side | Take them at night when it's dark | Shake the branch so they fall
exercise: Noah confessed, then weeks later took a rare swap card. What does this show? | People can slip back even after confession | He was never really sorry the first time | Confession never works | Swap cards don't count as taking
exercise: What did the priest say when Noah came back with a second wrong? | Everyone slips, forgiveness doesn't run out, but put it right each time | You can't confess the same kind of sin twice, so this one stays with you forever | It's fine now because you have confessed before | Stop coming to confession
exercise: Why did the whole swap club stop for a week? | One card was taken, so nobody felt safe to swap | The leader was ill and nobody else had the key | The cards were too old | The school closed the hall
exercise: A boy asked for a toy, Mum said no, and he took it from the shop anyway. Why is this two wrongs? | He disobeyed Mum and he stole from the shop | It is only one wrong, since he asked Mum first | Asking first makes it all right, whatever Mum says | It's no wrong at all if Mum finds it in the car
exercise: Mum found the toy hidden under the car seat. What was the right thing? | Go back, give it to the shop lady and say sorry himself | Let Mum take it back to the shop while he waits in the car | Keep it, since they've already left | Throw it out of the window
exercise: A boy takes Dad's phone charger to school and loses it. Dad misses a call at work. How could it have been put right? | Say sorry and pay part of a new one from his savings | Blame his teacher for losing it, since it was lost at school | Say the charger was old anyway | Buy Dad a sweet
exercise: Why do habits change slowly? | Old habits come back when you are bored or with the same friends | Habits change the moment you say sorry and mean it | Habits never change at all | Only grown-ups can change habits
exercise: A girl wants her friend's pen for a minute. What is the wise choice? | Ask, and take it only if the friend says yes | Take it and give it back before she notices it is gone | Take it if she has two | Take it if it is a cheap pen
exercise: Noah took apples on a dare. What did the neighbour say when he said sorry? | Next time just ask, and she gave him a bag of them | She called the police and told his parents | She never spoke to him again | She cut down the tree
exercise: Which helps most to stop slipping back into taking things? | Being less idle and asking first, every time | Promising to be perfect forever and ever | Never going outside | Making sure nobody can see you
exercise: Someone has more toys than you. Does that make it fair to take one? | No, having less doesn't make taking fair | Yes, if you give it back to them later on | Yes, if they won't ever notice that it's gone | Yes, if they are part of your own big family
test: Cousin's mum on the phone: «Has anyone seen Jack's red truck?» It's in Noah's bag. What should Noah do? | Give it back and say sorry himself | Say nothing and keep it hidden in his room | Say Jack must have lost it outside himself | Leave it in their garden at night secretly
test: «mate it's only borrowin, I'll put it back» – about a classmate's calculator, without asking. What is true? | Without asking, it's taking, not borrowing | It's fine if he puts it back after the lesson | It's fine between classmates | It's fine for one lesson
test: Swap club kid: «oi! who nicked my shiny card?» Noah has it. What makes it right? | Put it back, own up and swap only when both agree | Swap it for a worse one secretly, so the owner still has a card | Say it fell under the table | Say nothing and stop coming
test: Noah: «I already confessed nicking stuff once, so this time doesn't count». What is wrong with that? | Each new wrong needs owning up and putting right too | Nothing, one confession covers all the wrongs that come after | He should never confess twice | It only counts if he gets caught
test: Mate: «grab them apples, the old girl never looks». She does look, from her window. What would have been wiser? | Knock and ask her for some | Take them at dusk instead of now | Take only a few from the low branches | Climb the fence on the other side
test: Dad, annoyed: «My phone died at work. Have you got my charger?» The boy lost it at school. What is the honest answer? | «Yes, I took it and lost it. Sorry, I'll help pay» | «You must have left it at work, Dad, I saw you take it» | «No, I never touched it» | «Maybe the cat took it»
test: Mum in the car: «What's that under the seat? I said NO to that toy.» What should happen next? | They go back and he returns it and says sorry | He promises to pay for it later out of his pocket money and keeps it | He says a friend gave it to him | He says it fell into the bag by itself
test: Kid: «I took a book out without signing, nobody noticed, so I'll keep doing it». Why is that wrong? | Others can't find or borrow the book, and it isn't fair | It's fine if nobody noticed, the books all come back in the end | Libraries don't need records | Books come back by themselves
test: Which is TRUE about slipping back after saying sorry? | It happens to everyone, but each slip still has to be put right | It means you are a bad person forever and can never change | It means the first sorry was useless | It doesn't matter after the first time
test: «fountain money's just lying there, free cash». What is the right answer? | It's still somebody's money, usually for charity | Yes, it's free once it's in water | Only the big coins are owned | It belongs to the shopping centre's children
test: Swapper: «you can have my card if I can have yours?» Noah says yes. Why is this fine when taking wasn't? | Both boys agree, so it's a fair swap | Swaps never count | The card is cheaper now | It isn't fine either
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the removal of an article without the permission of its owner, with the intention of its subsequent return. | It constitutes taking without consent and is not properly described as borrowing | It is permissible provided the article is returned undamaged and within a reasonable time | It constitutes borrowing and is therefore entirely permissible | It is permissible where the owner possesses a surplus of such articles
exam: Identify the principal teaching conveyed by the priest to a penitent who confesses a repetition of a former wrong. | Forgiveness is not exhausted, but each wrong must be made good in turn | A repeated wrong may not be confessed on a second occasion and remains unforgiven | A wrong once confessed may thereafter be repeated without consequence | The penitent is relieved of all duty of restitution
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises fruit growing upon a branch which overhangs a neighbouring property? | It remains the property of the owner of the tree | It becomes the property of whoever stands beneath it | It may be taken freely by children | It belongs to no person whatsoever
exam: Ascertain why the taking of an article which a parent has expressly refused to purchase constitutes a twofold wrong. | It comprises both disobedience to the parent and theft from the shop | It comprises merely a single minor discourtesy | It is no wrong where the article is inexpensive | It is no wrong where the parent subsequently discovers it
